+# Decision: Concurrent Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers on tablets that can be offline for 48 hours sometimes correct the same visit independently. The record store must define what the current visit is when two or more submissions start from the same earlier state. The answer sets the submission envelope that tablet-sync stores and retries, the shape of the work coordinators review, and which visits review-export may include in the partner's Friday CSV. Reversing it after tablets are in the field changes all three.
+
+A visit's scientific fields are related in ways the server cannot read from field names. Correcting an observer name and adding a missing species are unrelated; revising a species identification and its cover estimate are one observation.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates**
+
+- **No original submission is discarded** (inherited from the HLD tenet *Do not silently discard a submitted edit*; REC-004). Both options retain every original submission.
+
+**Weighted criteria**
+
+- **Scientific coherence of the current visit — major.** A current or exported visit should not join fields from different workers in a way no one reviewed. Derives from the HLD non-goal of inferring scientific intent and the tenet *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*.
+- **Coordinator review workload — major.** Two coordinators resolve questions, and unresolved visits must be cleared before Friday. Derives from the tenet *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*.
+- **Maintenance burden of the rules in play — moderate.** The same tenet applies to rules that someone must keep correct as the survey protocol changes.
+- **Tablet complexity and submission size — moderate.** Tablets keep local drafts across restarts and multi-day disconnection.
+- **Share of visits export-ready without review — moderate.** Derives from the goal of making reviewable problems visible before the Friday export.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Each submission is a complete visit and names the revision it started from. When two or more accepted submissions share a starting revision, the visit has competing complete revisions and no single current state. A coordinator compares the revisions and resolves them. Unresolved visits are held out of the export.
+
+- **Original submissions retained** — passes. Every submission is stored as a revision.
+- **Scientific coherence** — strong. Every current or exported visit is a state some worker submitted whole or a coordinator chose; fields from different workers are never combined without a person looking.
+- **Coordinator review workload** — weak. Any two edits from the same starting state create review work, including edits to unrelated fields.
+- **Maintenance burden** — strong. There are no merge rules; the server compares base revision IDs.
+- **Tablet complexity and submission size** — strong. A tablet retains base revision IDs and sends whole visits. It must show pending competing revisions.
+- **Export-ready share** — weak. Every visit with concurrent edits waits for a coordinator.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Each submission supplies its base state and its structured changes. Changes to disjoint fields combine automatically into the current visit; changes that overlap go to a coordinator.
+
+- **Original submissions retained** — passes. Submissions are stored; the combined record is derived.
+- **Scientific coherence** — partial. Combination is sound only as far as the rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries capture the relationships between fields. A combination across a relationship the rules miss is a current visit no one reviewed.
+- **Coordinator review workload** — strong. Only overlapping changes are reviewed.
+- **Maintenance burden** — weak. Merge rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries must be maintained, and field-level provenance must be kept so a combined value stays inspectable.
+- **Tablet complexity and submission size** — partial. A tablet keeps base snapshots and sends structured changes.
+- **Export-ready share** — strong. Visits with only disjoint edits need no review.
+
+## Selection
+
+Whole-visit revisions.
+
+Scientific coherence outweighs review workload. The record store cannot know which edits are scientifically related, and a combined record that looks fine but joins related edits is a wrong observation that reaches the partner without anyone having reviewed it. A larger review queue is visible and clearable; an unnoticed combination is not. The option also avoids a rule set that needs maintenance by coordinators without a systems administrator. It gives up export-ready share and review workload, a cost the project accepts.
+
+**When to reconsider.** Field-aware merging is not ruled out in principle. Revisit if review volume measured over a season shows coordinators cannot clear competing revisions before the Friday export, and the unrelated-field edits that make up most of the queue can be identified by a small, auditable set of rules. A revisit would require the combined record to remain inspectable per field and would likely keep a manual-approval step for combinations.
+
+**Obligations on tablet-sync.** Keep the base revision ID with each local draft for as long as the draft exists, across restarts and disconnection. Send complete visits with the base revision ID and idempotency key. Show a field worker when a visit they edited has competing revisions, and what the competing revisions are. The retry state machine must treat a base revision ID as immutable for a draft.
+
+**Obligations on review-export.** Provide a comparison view of competing complete revisions for each unresolved visit, showing authors and receipt times, with the shared earlier revision as reference. Support resolution as defined by record-store, recording the coordinator and contributing revisions. Exclude unresolved visits from the CSV, and make the held-out visits visible before the Friday export. Trace an exported row to the revision and, where resolved, the resolution that produced it.
